\id JON E2T (Easy to Translate Bible) 
\h Jonah 
\toc1 This book is the account about Jonah, when he disobeyed God, was three days in a big fish's belly, and finally went to warn the wicked people in Nineveh city that God was going to destroy them. We call this book JONAH. 
\toc2 Jonah 
\toc3 JON 
\mt2 This book is the account about Jonah, when he disobeyed God, was three days in a big fish's belly, and finally went to warn the wicked people in Nineveh city that God was going to destroy them. We call this book  
\mt1 JONAH 
\c 1  
\s1 Jonah refuses to obey Yahweh 
\p  
\v 1 \add  One day\add* Yahweh said to the \add prophet\add* Jonah, Amittai's son,   
\v 2   "I have seen how wicked are \add the people who live in\add* the great city called Nineveh. Therefore, go there and warn the people that I am planning to destroy their city because they d sinned." \f *One day* Yahweh said to the *prophet *Jonah, Amittai’s son  \ft OR *There was a prophet in Israel named* Jonah who was Amittai's son. *One day* Yahweh said to him: \f*\f *One day* Yahweh said to the *prophet *Jonah, Amittai’s son  \ft Use words that will indicate that this is a real, historical story. Do not use words that might communicate that this is a folk tale, a fake story or a myth. \f*\f the great city called Nineveh \ft OR the large/important city called Nineveh* in the Assyria kingdom* \f*\f the great city called Nineveh \ft Jonah 3:1 \f* 
\p  
\v 3   But \add Jonah\add* did not go \add to Nineveh\add*. \add Instead, he\add* went in the direction opposite to where Yahweh told him to go. He went (down) to Joppa \add city\add*. There he paid the fare to travel on a ship that was going to Tarshish \add city\add*. Jonah wanted to escape from Yahweh and avoid doing what Yahweh had told him to do. He got on the ship, \add and then he went down to a lower deck inside the ship. He lay down, and went to sleep.\add*\f went (down)  \ft OR went   OR traveled  \f*\f went (down)  \ft Jonah had to travel from a high elevation (Jerusalem city) down to Joppa city which was a low elevation. Joppa was at sea level where a ship could be docked.The repeated “down” communicates that Jonah is going down from Yahweh’s exalted/”up” position. \f* 
\p  
\v 4   Then Yahweh caused a very strong wind to blow. There was such a violent storm that \add the sailors thought\add* the ship would break apart.   
\v 5   The sailors were very frightened. So, they each started to pray to their own gods \add to save them\add*. Then they threw the cargo into the sea to make the ship lighter \add in order that it would not sink easily\add*. \f they each started to pray to their own gods \ft OR each one started to pray to his own god \f* 
\p  
\v 6   Then the captain went \add down\add* to where Jonah was sleeping deeply. He \add awoke him and\add* said to him. "You should not be sleeping \add during a storm like this one!\add* Get up and pray to your god! Perhaps he will pity us and save us, so that we will not drown!"   
\p  
\v 7   Then the sailors said to each other, "We must cast lots to determine who has caused all this trouble!" So they did that, and the lots showed that Jonah had caused the storm.   
\p  
\v 8   So \add various ones among\add* them asked Jonah, "Are you the one who has caused all this trouble for us?" "What work do you do?" "Where are you coming from?" "What country and what people-group do you belong to?" \f cast lots \ft OR throw objects/lots/dice OR shake in a container small objects that we have all marked and then let one fall out \f*\f cast lots \ft Proverbs 16.33; Acts 1:26 \f*\f caused all this trouble \ft OR offended the god that caused the storm \f*\f the lots showed that Jonah had caused the storm \ft OR the dice identified Jonah OR  the *object *with Jonah’s mark *fell out from the bag/container* \f* 
\p  
\v 9   Jonah replied, "I am a Hebrew. I worship Yahweh, who lives in heaven. He is the God who made the sea and the land. I am trying to escape from \add doing what\add* Yahweh \add told me to do\add*." \f I am a Hebrew \ft OR I am from the Hebrew people-group. \f* 
\p  
\v 10   After/When the sailors heard that, they were \add more\add* terrified. So, they asked him, "Do you realize what trouble you have caused?"  
\p  
\v 11   The storm kept getting worse, and the waves kept getting bigger. So, one sailor among them asked Jonah, "What must we do \add to you, so that your god\add* will make the sea become calm?"  
\p  
\v 12   Jonah replied to them, "Pick me up and throw me into the sea. \add If you do that,\add* then the sea will become calm. I know that this terrible storm is caused by my \add not doing what Yahweh told me to do\add*."  
\p  
\v 13   But the sailors \add did not want to do that. Instead,\add* they tried hard to row the ship back to land. But \add they could not do so, because\add* the storm continued to get worse. \f *did not want to do that* \ft OR did not want to throw Jonah into the sea \f*\f row the ship \ft OR pull the ship OR paddle the ship OR get the ship \f* 
\p  
\v 14   Therefore, the sailors prayed to Yahweh, "O Yahweh, please do not cause us to drown after we cause this man to die. O Yahweh, we beg you, do not consider us to be guilty when we cause this man to die. We know that you have sent this storm for your own good reasons." \f you have sent this storm for your own good reasons \ft OR you have done what you wanted to do \f* 
\p  
\v 15   Then they picked Jonah up and threw him into the sea. Then the sea became calm.   
\v 16   When that happened, the sailors became greatly awed at/respected Yahweh's power. So, they offered a sacrifice to Yahweh. They also vowed to him that they would do things that would please him. \f became greatly awed at Yahweh’s power \ft OR greatly respected Yahweh’s power \f* 
\p  
\v 17 \add  While they were doing that,\add* Yahweh sent a huge fish that swallowed Jonah. Then Jonah was inside the fish's belly for three days and three nights. \f that swallowed Jonah \ft *OR that swallowed Jonah alive OR who put Jonah in his mouth and downed him* \f*\f that swallowed Jonah \ft The fish swallowed Jonah without chewing or biting him. The fish did not eat Jonah. \f*\f for three days and three nights \ft OR three days \f*\f for three days and three nights \ft Matthew 12:40 \f* 
\c 2  
\s2 Jonah thanks Yahweh for rescuing him 
\p  
\v 1   While Jonah was inside the huge fish, he prayed to Yahweh God, \add whom he worshiped\add*. \add This is what Jonah reported later:\add*\f *This is what Jonah reported later:   * \ft OR (omit) \f* 
\p  
\v 2   Jonah said, "Yahweh, when I was greatly distressed there \add in the fish belly\add*, I prayed to you, and you heard what I prayed. When I was about to descend way down into the place where dead people go, you heard me when I called out for you to help/save me.  \f Jonah said \ft OR  Jonah prayed \f*\f Jonah said \ft Jonah’s prayer is a Hebrew poem or song.  If you want to  create a poem in your language from the content of Jonah’s prayer, or sing Jonah’s song in your language, that would be very appropriate \f*\f about to descend way down into the place where dead people go \ft OR when I almost died  OR when I was as good as dead \f* 
\v 3   You threw me down into the deep water, down to the sea's bottom. The sea currents swirled around me, and the huge waves crashed above me.  \f You threw me \ft OR You caused men to throw me \f*\f the huge waves crashed above me \ft OR You buried me under crashing waves \f* 
\v 4   Then I thought/said, 'Although You have banished me, Yet I will go to see your holy temple again!' \f Then I thought/said, ‘Although You \ft OR Then I thought that although You  \f*\f Then I thought/said, ‘Although You \ft Jonah 2:7 \f*\f Then I thought/said, ‘Although You \ft Since Jonah is thinking and not speaking out loud, in your translation, you might want to use an indirect quote rather than a direct speech quote. \f*\f You have banished me \ft OR You have expelled/cast me away from your presence/sight \f*\f Yet I will \ft OR But surely I will  \f*\f Yet I will \ft There is a text translation issue with this part of verse 4.  The Hebrew text introduces Jonah’s second thought with a word meaning ‘yet’ or ‘surely’, expressing hope. But there is one alternate Greek translation from the Hebrew that uses a word meaning ‘how’, expressing a sense of hopelessness, that Jonah might never again see the Lord.  English versions are divided in how they render this meaning.  Many follow the second alternative, even though it has little textual support.  So, it is recommended that you follow the Hebrew text and the options given here.  (TN Jonah) \f*\f to see your holy temple again \ft OR to see the holy place where you/Yahweh live \f*\f to see your holy temple again \ft There is disagreement among scholars concerning whether the temple mentioned in chapter 2, verses 4 and 7, refers to the earthly temple in Jerusalem, or to Yahweh’s eternal dwelling place in heaven. So, if possible, avoid referring to the location of the temple in these verses. In both verses, the sense seems to be “the holy place where you (the Lord) live.” (TN Jonah, Hohulin, 2000)Both the Jerusalem temple and the temple in heaven were true dwelling places for Yahweh. The Glory of Yahweh Himself used to dwell in the tabernacle and later in the temple. Jonah had “come down” from Jerusalem to Joppa, and contemplated returning to Jerusalem. In the context of Jewish “cosmic geography,” Judah was Yahweh’s land, and Nineveh was a dwelling place for demons.  GC \f* 
\p  
\v 5   "The water surrounded me, and threatened to drown me. Seaweed was wrapped around my head.  \f seaweed \ft OR sea plants \f* 
\v 6   I sank down to where the mountains start rising from the sea bottom. I thought that it would be as though my body would forever be in a prison inside the earth below me. But you, Yahweh God, whom I worship, you rescued me from going down to the place where the dead go.  \f rescued me from going down to the place where the dead go \ft OR rescued me from death   OR rescued me back from dying \f*\f rescued me from going down to the place where the dead go \ft We do not know if Jonah died physically or only metaphorically (figuratively speaking). \f* 
\v 7   When I was almost dead, Yahweh, I thought about you. You heard my prayer \add to you\add*, up there where you are, in your holy temple.  \f When I was almost dead \ft OR As I was dying \f*\f When I was almost dead \ft Jonah 2:4 \f* 
\v 8   All those who worship worthless idols are rejecting you, the one who could act kindly toward them.  \f who could act kindly \ft *OR who is faithful to keep his promises * \f*\f who could act kindly \ft *The Hebrew word here is khesed*. (Here rendered kindly) The common meaning of the Hebrew term *khesed *seems to include loyalty or reliability, faithfulness and kindness or steady compassion. \f* 
\v 9   But I will sing to thank you, and I will offer a sacrifice to thank you, Yahweh. I will surely do what I have solemnly promised to do. Yahweh, you are the only one who is able to save us." \f But I \ft OR But, as for me, I \f*\f offer a sacrifice to thank you \ft OR offer to sacrifice animals to thank you \f*\f offer a sacrifice to thank you \ft In ancient times, pagans commonly offered sacrifices to bribe their gods or to placate them. In Israel, however, sacrifices were for purifying dirty/polluted things, worshiping and honoring Yahweh, and expressing thanks to Yahweh.  \f*\f Yahweh, you are the only one who is able to save us \ft OR Because it is Yahweh alone who saves anyone.      \f* 
\c 3  
\s3 The people in Nineveh repented after Jonah warned them 
\p  
\v 1   Then Yahweh said to Jonah a second time, saying,   
\v 2   "Go immediately to that great city called Nineveh, and tell the people who live there the message that I gave you \add previously\add*." \f that great city \ft OR   that large/important city \f*\f that great city \ft Jonah 1:2 \f*\f that great city \ft How you described the city in Jonah 1:2, be consistent to describe it the same way here.   \f* 
\p  
\v 3   So, \add this time\add* Jonah obeyed Yahweh, and he went to Nineveh. That city was so big that \add a person\add*\add had to walk\add* for three days to \add go\add*\add everywhere in\add* it. \f a person had to walk for three days to go everywhere in it \ft OR  it took three days to walk through it OR it took three days for a person to visit/see it all \f*\f a person had to walk for three days to go everywhere in it \ft The walls surrounding Nineveh extended five kilometers around, so a person could walk around the city in a few hours. Jonah’s three-day walk likely refers to walking about inside the city. The number three is important to retain in this story. \f* 
\p  
\v 4   On the first day \add after\add* Jonah \add arrived\add*, he \add began walking\add* through the city. He started proclaiming to the people, "Forty days from now, \add God\add* will destroy Nineveh!"   
\p  
\v 5   The people in Nineveh believed God's \add message\add*. They \add all\add* decided that everyone should stop eating food, and they must dress themselves with old/rough clothes, \add to show that they were sorry for having sinned\add*.  So, \add everyone\add*, including important people and unimportant people, \add did so\add*. \f stop eating food \ft OR fast  OR abstain from food \f* 
\p  
\v 6   Nineveh's king heard the news about Jonah and \add what the people were doing\add*. \add So,\add* he took off his royal robes, and \add he also\add* put on old/rough clothes. He left his palace, and sat down on \add cold\add* ashes, \add to humble himself and show that he also was sorry for having sinned\add*.   
\v 7   Then he sent messengers to proclaim to \add everyone in\add* Nineveh:  
\p   "My advisors and I have decreed that no one may eat or drink anything. Do not even allow your animals to eat or drink.   
\v 8   Instead, every person must put on old/rough cloth. \add Put old/rough cloth on\add* your animals, \add too\add*. Then everyone must pray fervently to God. And everyone must stop doing evil actions/things, and stop acting violently \add toward others\add*.   
\v 9   Perhaps, \add if everyone does so\add*, then God may change his mind and be merciful \add to\add* us.  Maybe God will stop being very angry with us, with the result that we will not die."  
\p  
\v 10   When they \add all did that\add*, God saw what they were doing, and he saw that they had stopped doing evil things. So, \add he pitied them, and\add* he did not destroy them as he had threatened to do.   
\c 4  
\s4 Yahweh teaches Jonah that he has/shows mercy and compassion 
\p  
\v 1   But when Yahweh did not punish Nineveh, this distressed/displeased Jonah very much! And he became very angry. \add (The people in Nineveh were not Jews, and Jonah did not think that God should act mercifully toward people who were enemies to the Jews.)\add* 
\p  
\v 2   So, Jonah complained to Yahweh, saying: "O Yahweh, what you have done is what I thought that you would do, before I left home. That is why I \add decided\add* immediately to run away, and go to Tarshish \add city\add*. I did so because I knew that you, O God, act very kindly and compassionate \add toward all people\add*. You do not quickly become angry with people who do evil things. You love people very much, and you change your mind about punishing \add sinful\add*\add people\add*\add when they repent\add*.  \f complained \ft OR prayed [with bitterness] \f*\f You love people very much \ft OR You keep your promises to people \f*\f You love people very much \ft The Hebrew word *khesed *remains the foremost characteristic for God, meaning covenant loyalty. The Greek equivalent is “faithful(ness)”. \f*\f you, O God, act very kindly and compassionate *toward all people*. You do not quickly become angry with people who do evil things. You love people very much, and you change your mind about punishing *sinful people when they repent*. \ft Exodus 34:6; Jeremiah 18:1-11 \f*\f you, O God, act very kindly and compassionate *toward all people*. You do not quickly become angry with people who do evil things. You love people very much, and you change your mind about punishing *sinful people when they repent*. \ft Jonah is summarizing a passage about Yahweh that is repeated very often throughout Scripture. \f*\f allow me to die \ft OR kill me now \f*\f It is not right/good for you to be angry about my not destroying the city \ft OR Is it right for you to be angry about my not destroying the city?   \f*\f It is not right/good for you to be angry about my not destroying the city \ft In Hebrew, this is a rhetorical question, not a real question. You may translate it either as a strong statement, or as a rhetorical question that is designed to make the listener/reader think. \f* 
\v 3   So, now, O Yahweh, \add because you will not destroy Nineveh city as you said you would,\add* allow me to die. It is better for me to die than to \add continue to\add* live."  
\p  
\v 4   Yahweh replied, "Why are you angry \add that I have not destroyed the city\add*?" \f It is better for me to die than to *continue to* live \ft OR I do not want to live. I want to die. \f*\f Why are you angry  \ft OR Is it right for you to be angry OR There is no good reason for  you to be angry OR It is not right/good for you to be angry \f*\f Why are you angry  \ft This is a rhetorical question which Yahweh is “asking” Jonah in order to rebuke him and make him change his bad attitude.  You may translate it as a statement if that is a more meaningful way for your language. \f*\f *not destroyed* \ft *OR spared OR saved* \f* 
\p  
\v 5   Jonah \add did not reply\add*. He went out from the city to its east \add side\add*. He made a small shelter so that he could sit under it and be protected from the sun. He sat under the shelter and waited to see what would happen to the city.  
\p  
\v 6   Then Yahweh God caused a vine to grow up \add immediately\add* to shade Jonah's head from the sun and make him \add feel\add* more comfortable. Jonah was very happy about \add having\add* the vine \add over his head\add*.  \f He went out from the city to its east *side* \ft OR He went out through the main gate and sat down near the city (*where he could overlook the city) *    \f*\f He went out from the city to its east *side* \ft The Hebrew word *qedem* may mean ‘front’ or ‘east’. (NET2 note)There is a theme in Scripture, that when people go “east”, this indicates that they are going into exile or separating themselves from Yahweh. \f* 
\v 7   But before dawn the next day, God sent a worm that chewed the vine, with the result that the vine withered.   
\v 8   Then, when the sun rose \add high in the sky\add*, God sent a very hot wind from the east. The sun shone very strongly on Jonah's head, with the result that he felt faint. He wanted to die, and he said, "It would be better for me to die than to \add continue to\add* live!" \f a worm \ft OR an insect  OR a bug \f* 
\p  
\v 9   But God asked Jonah, "Is it right/good for you to be angry \add because the vine died\add*?" \f Is it right for you to be angry because the plant died? \ft OR You have no right to be angry just because this plant died/withered  OR You should not be angry that the plant withered! \f*\f Is it right for you to be angry because the plant died? \ft This may be a rhetorical question, not a real question. \f*\f Yes, it is right!  Now I am very angry and I want to die! \ft OR I am right to be angry so much that I wish I were dead. \f* 
\p  
\v 10   But Yahweh said \add to him\add*, "You feel sorry about my \add causing\add* that vine \add to wither\add*, even though you did not make it grow and you did not care for it. It just grew up during one night, and it completely withered during the next night.   
\v 11   But there are more than 120,000 people in Nineveh who do not know right from wrong \add (and they are more valuable than that vine)\add*. There are also many animals/livestock/cows \add in Nineveh\add*. So, it is right for me to show compassion to the people who live in that huge city \add and not want to destroy them!\add*" \f who do not know right from wrong \ft OR who do not know that they are living not knowing about My laws/ways OR who do not know the difference between their right hand and their left hand \f*\f who do not know right from wrong \ft The Hebrew here says literally, “people who do not know the difference between their right and left hands”. Some scholars think this is referring to children. That is possible, but this is probably an idiom meaning that the people, no matter their age, have no discernment, no education, or no knowledge about God’s teaching on what is true and what is not true. Translate it so that it communicates a clear meaning.  \f*\f there are also many animals/livestock/cows \ft OR there are also many herds/cattle (*and none among these have done anything to displease me.*)  \f*\f  it is right for me \ft OR Is it not right/good for me OR Therefore I should \f*\f  it is right for me \ft This may be a rhetorical question or a real question that Yahweh asks Jonah, so that Jonah will reconsider and repent of his bad attitude. (God is making a strong distinction between Jonah's attitude and his behavior.) But we do not hear Jonah’s response. The book intentionally ends with this question or statement, so that people hearing this book will think about it for themselves. \f*\f show compassion to \ft OR feel sorry for \f*\f + \ft Passage Overview: Once again, Yahweh commands his prophet Jonah to go to Israel’s enemies In Nineveh city. He was supposed to preach to them to repent so that God  would not destroy them.  This time, Jonah obeys the Lord, but he is not happy when the Ninevites repent and Yahweh does not destroy them. Jonah is angry that the city is saved, so Yahweh sends a hot wind, a leafy vine and a worm to try and reason with Jonah about his bad attitude and lack of compassion for people.  Background Info: Historical context: Prominent Themes: \f*\f + \ft ● The pagan kingdom of Assyria, of which Nineveh was the capitol, was an enemy nation to Israel. Assyria raided and abused other nations. Nineveh was directly north of Israel, accessible by land travel.  \f*\f + \ft ● (2Kings 14:25) Jonah, a Jewish prophet of Yahweh, was alive during the reign of Jeroboam II, king of Israel, about 700 years before Jesus' birth. ● Sometime after Jonah's story, about 615 B.C., the prophet Nahum prophesied the downfall of Assyria and Nineveh (Nahum 1-3)..  ● In 612 B.C. God destroyed Nineveh for their wicked ways. (Their repentance did not last long.) \f*\f + \ft ● God wants people in all nations to repent and so to be saved. ● God is compassionate and patient.  ● Psalm 135:6 shows God’s sovereign power by using/commanding, for his purposes, big and small creatures, plants and human devices for discerning (i.e., casting lots), the sea and wind, wicked people, to teach people about himself. ● Jeremiah 18:7-10 shows how God can and does change his mind (repent) about doing what he said he would do depending on people’s actions, responses and prayers.  \f*\f + \ft Passage Overview: Yahweh commands his prophet Jonah to go to Israel’s enemies In Nineveh city. He was supposed to preach to them to repent so that God  would not destroy them.  Jonah refuses Yahweh’s command and tries to run away, going in the opposite direction.  Yahweh sends storms, people and animals to redirect Jonah, causing him to repent and obey.  Background Info: Historical context: Prominent Themes: \f*\f + \ft ● Jonah was thrown into the ocean and a large fish swallowed him alive. Now he prays to Yahweh from inside the fish. \f*\f + \ft ● Jonah is inside a large fish for 3 days and 3 nights praying/repenting, and then Yahweh causes him to be “raised/resurrected” back to life which will be a signal for Messiah \f*\f + \ft ● God is compassionate and patient.  ● God spoke through Jonah's words, and he also used Jonah's life as a prophetic message to signal the identity of the promised Messiah (Matt. 12:40). \f*\f + \ft Passage Overview: Yahweh commands his prophet Jonah to go to Israel’s enemies In Nineveh city. He was supposed to preach to them to repent so that God  would not destroy them.  Jonah refuses Yahweh’s command and tries to run away, going in the opposite direction.  Yahweh sends storms, people and animals to redirect Jonah, causing him to repent and obey.  Background Info: Historical context: Prominent Themes: \f*\f + \ft ● The pagan kingdom of Assyria, for which Nineveh was the capitol, was an enemy nation to Israel. Assyria raided and abused other nations. Nineveh was directly north of Israel, accessible by land travel.  ● Tarshish was far to the west of Israel, accessible only by boat travel, across the Mediterranean Sea. \f*\f + \ft ● (2Kings 14:25) Jonah, a Jewish prophet of Yahweh, was alive during the reign of Jeroboam II, king of Israel, about 700 years before Jesus' birth. ● Sometime after Jonah's story, about 615 B.C., the prophet Nahum prophesied the downfall of Assyria and Nineveh (Nahum 1-3)..  ● In 612 B.C. God destroyed Nineveh for their wicked ways. (Their repentance did not last long.) \f*\f + \ft ● down versus up; the chaotic sea versus the “safe” dry land ● God wants people in all nations to repent and so to be saved. ● God is compassionate and patient.  ● God spoke through Jonah's words, and he also used Jonah's life as a prophetic message to signal the identity of the promised Messiah (Matt. 12:40). ● Psalm 135:6 shows God’s sovereign power by using/commanding, for his purposes, big and small creatures, plants and human devices for discerning (i.e., casting lots), the sea and wind, wicked people, to teach people about himself. ● Jeremiah 18:7-10 shows how God can and does change his mind (repent) about doing what he said he would do depending on people’s actions, responses and prayers.  \f*\f + \ft Passage Overview: Once again, Yahweh commands his prophet Jonah to go to Israel’s enemies In Nineveh city. He was supposed to preach to them to repent so that God  would not destroy them.  This time, Jonah obeys the Lord, but he is not happy when the Ninevites repent and Yahweh does not destroy them. Jonah is angry that the city is saved, so Yahweh sends a hot wind, a leafy vine and a worm to try and reason with Jonah about his bad attitude and lack of compassion for people.  Background Info: Historical context: Prominent Themes: \f*\f + \ft ● The pagan kingdom of Assyria, of which Nineveh was the capitol, was an enemy nation to Israel. Assyria raided and abused other nations. Nineveh was directly north of Israel, accessible by land travel.  \f*\f + \ft ● Sometime after Jonah's story, about 615 B.C., the prophet Nahum prophesied the downfall of Assyria and Nineveh (Nahum 1-3)..  ● In 612 B.C. God destroyed Nineveh for their wicked ways. (Their repentance did not last long.) \f*\f + \ft ● God wants people in all nations to repent and so to be saved. ● God is compassionate and patient.  ● Psalm 135:6 shows God’s sovereign power by using/commanding, for his purposes, big and small creatures, plants and human devices for discerning (i.e., casting lots), the sea and wind, wicked people, to teach people about himself. ● As in Jonah, Jeremiah 18:7-10 shows how God can and does change his mind (repent) about doing what he said he would do depending on people’s actions, responses and prayers.  \f*